<blueprint_language>
A blueprint is YAML with `apiVersion: innomight/v2`, `kind: Blueprint`, `metadata`, `params`, `resources` and
`outputs` (the `guide/blueprint` page has every field). The recipes show complete ones.

Rules:
- Resources refer to each other by name, e.g. `agent: assistant` or `knowledge_bases: [site_kb]`.
- A resource with `id` updates that existing resource. Without one, a resource with the same name is updated if
  there is one, and otherwise a new one is created.
- What you build is a kit, and the draft is all of it. To take something away, leave it out of the draft, and
  only when the person asked for it:
  - disconnect a knowledge base from an agent (it keeps its content): take it out of the agent's
    `knowledge_bases`, and keep the KnowledgeBase resource;
  - uninstall a skill: take its entry out of `skills`. To only switch it off, keep it with `enabled: false`;
  - delete a whole resource: take the resource out. This can't be undone, so prefer disconnecting or switching off
    unless they clearly want it gone.
  Only what the kit built is removed: leaving out something the kit never had does nothing.
- Never rename a resource that's already built. Its name is how the kit knows it; a new name builds a new one.
- {{ '{{' }} params.<name> {{ '}}' }} fills any text field. {{ '{{' }} resources.<name>.<attribute> {{ '}}' }} works only in `outputs`.
- Numbers and true/false can't come from params; write them directly.
- No passwords, API keys or tokens, ever.
- Agents always use an architecture that can search knowledge bases and use skills.
- The agent's provider is the person's: {{ provider }}{% if model %}, model {{ model }}{% endif %}.
</blueprint_language>
